1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,680
 If you ever wanted to use VI on the command line

2
00:00:04,680 --> 00:00:07,040
 in order to navigate your history,

3
00:00:07,040 --> 00:00:09,420
 a lot of people don't know about Set-Show VI.

4
00:00:09,420 --> 00:00:10,980
 I just reminded somebody about it.

5
00:00:10,980 --> 00:00:13,740
 They're like, oh my god, Set-Show is basically VI.

6
00:00:13,740 --> 00:00:15,420
 This is not the default, for whatever reason,

7
00:00:15,420 --> 00:00:18,200
 probably because Richard Solomon made Bash.

8
00:00:18,200 --> 00:00:21,840
 They made Emacs mode the default, which, yes, this is true.

9
00:00:21,840 --> 00:00:23,560
 If you can do Emacs.

10
00:00:23,560 --> 00:00:27,960
 And you can operate on the command line history file

11
00:00:27,960 --> 00:00:32,600
 as if it were a file you were editing using Emacs or VI.

12
00:00:32,600 --> 00:00:33,960
 You don't have to use zero keys, zero.

13
00:00:33,960 --> 00:00:35,200
 People are like, don't you use zero keys?

14
00:00:35,200 --> 00:00:36,840
 I'm like, no, I never use zero keys for anything.

15
00:00:36,840 --> 00:00:38,720
 I use VI keys for everything.

16
00:00:38,720 --> 00:00:40,920
 So, in fact, almost the first thing I ever do

17
00:00:40,920 --> 00:00:43,280
 whenever I get on any terminal is set to actual VI

18
00:00:43,280 --> 00:00:44,800
 so that I can use VI things.

19
00:00:44,800 --> 00:00:47,280
 And that way, when I do VI things,

20
00:00:47,280 --> 00:00:49,000
 so I just did control, I did escape,

21
00:00:49,000 --> 00:00:52,640
 and now I'm pushing K to go up and J to go down.

22
00:00:52,640 --> 00:00:54,160
 And you can do really amazing things

23
00:00:54,160 --> 00:00:56,400
 like you can search through your entire history

24
00:00:56,400 --> 00:00:58,080
 with a slash, right?

25
00:00:58,080 --> 00:01:00,480
 So, I regularly will have something

26
00:01:00,480 --> 00:01:02,280
 like I was pushing some stuff earlier.

27
00:01:02,280 --> 00:01:03,560
 I'll go find my push command,

28
00:01:03,560 --> 00:01:05,480
 and it jumps entirely through my history.

29
00:01:05,480 --> 00:01:08,400
 And it finds the one that has what that command was,

30
00:01:08,400 --> 00:01:10,160
 or I can do slash.

31
00:01:10,160 --> 00:01:11,280
 Whoops, I have to escape.

32
00:01:11,280 --> 00:01:13,840
 I have to do another one.

33
00:01:13,840 --> 00:01:15,960
 Fishies, so I go search for fishies.

34
00:01:15,960 --> 00:01:19,320
 So, everything else that you know about VI is command mode,

35
00:01:19,320 --> 00:01:20,520
 which is very powerful.

36
00:01:20,520 --> 00:01:23,080
 The more better you are with VI,

37
00:01:23,080 --> 00:01:24,840
 the better, the more time you spend in command mode,

38
00:01:24,840 --> 00:01:27,160
 the less time in edit mode,

39
00:01:27,160 --> 00:01:30,160
 and you never, ever, ever use your arrows.

40
00:01:30,160 --> 00:01:32,960
 So, this is a good way for you to do this.

41
00:01:32,960 --> 00:01:35,680
 It's also, a lot of other people will say,

42
00:01:35,680 --> 00:01:36,920
 you should be doing this and all that.

43
00:01:36,920 --> 00:01:38,160
 No, screw that.

44
00:01:38,160 --> 00:01:40,720
 I'm just gonna go search through and find

45
00:01:40,720 --> 00:01:43,520
 where I'm just gonna do a keyword search for everything,

46
00:01:43,520 --> 00:01:47,440
 staying very search-centric as you should.

